WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.340
CMake can do much more than compile existing source files.

2
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:13.080
It can run code generators, template expansion, resource compilers, protocol compilers, packaging tools, and project-specific scripts.

3
00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:26.060
That build-time execution is security-relevant because it can read files, write generated headers, create source, transform resources, and influence what eventually becomes part of the application.

4
00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:28.580
Generated files should have clear provenance.

5
00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:34.920
Reviewers should understand which input files, generator versions, and command options produced them.

6
00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:41.660
If generated code is checked into the repository, the team should know when and how it is refreshed.

7
00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:49.360
If generated code is produced only during the build, the command should be reproducible from reviewed inputs and should not depend on uncontrolled local state.

8
00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:53.880
Custom commands benefit from narrow, explicit declarations.

9
00:00:53.980 --> 00:01:00.860
Inputs, outputs, byproducts, working directories, dependencies, and comments should be accurate.

10
00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:08.400
That precision helps CMake and the underlying build tool know when work is required, and it helps humans understand the purpose of the step.

11
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:15.780
Broad script entry points that hide file discovery, path decisions, or generated outputs make review harder.

12
00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:20.860
Quoting and path handling are practical security concerns in build systems.

13
00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:26.560
Paths may contain spaces, unusual characters, or platform-specific separators.

14
00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:33.140
Commands should use CMake facilities that preserve argument boundaries and avoid accidental interpretation by another layer.

15
00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:41.480
The defensive mindset is simple: make generated behavior observable, deterministic, and tied to the project inputs the team intentionally approved.
